  "overview": {
    "id": "overview",
    "x": 108,
    "y": 80,
    "width": 1400,
    "height": 150,
    "layer": -3,
    "z": 129,
    "movable": false,
    "movableInEdit": false,
    "css": "font-size: 25px; ",
    "html": "A line widget is available from the Add Widget overlay in the &quotDecorative&quot section. Options include circles and lines with and without default stop holders. A line will be a graphical element that can be the parent for holders (usually) or other widgets that follow the line (or a curve) and whose connected points move together. The line itself can also act like a holder, which is especially useful when adding and removing stops."
  },
  "text1": {
    "id": "text1",
    "x": 10,
    "y": 400,
    "width": 360,
    "z": 185,
    "movable": false,
    "movableInEdit": false,
    "css": {
      "font-size": "22px"
    },
    "html": "The line above is the basic line added to the room. The line contains 2 stop points (the white circles) which are holders that by default will hold anything (such as the red cube). The line itself does not act as a holder by default, but you can change the <b>dropTarget</b> (the <b>Valid widgets</b> section of the &quotEdit Widgets&quot tab) and add widgets as stops. Look in the JSON Editor and you will see two line-specific properties: <b>lineEnd</b> and <b>lineStart</b>, but these are relative to the line's x/y position. If you select the line in Edit Mode, you will see 2 blue rings: the <b>start point</b> and the <b>end point</b>. Use your mouse to drag those around to resize the line. Changing <b>width</b> or <b>height</b> directly will not give the expected results. The <b>Open line / Closed shape</b> switch in the same tab turns a line into a circle or oval.",
    "layer": -3
  },
  "text2": {
    "id": "text2",
    "x": 437,
    "y": 400,
    "width": 300,
    "z": 121,
    "movable": false,
    "movableInEdit": false,
    "css": {
      "font-size": "22px"
    },
    "html": "The line above contains several new properties all easily added through the &quotEdit Widgets&quot tab. <b>lineColor</b> is the color of the path and <b>lineWidth</b> its thickness in pixels (<b>Line color</b>, <b>Line width</b>). <b>lineDash</b> (<b>Line style</b>) follows the syntax for CSS stroke-dasharray: the first number is the length of the dash, the second the space between dashes (in pixels). <b>controlEnd</b> and <b>controlStart</b> are the small orange squares connected by lines (only visible in edit mode) used to change the shape of the curve; the <b>Line shape</b> buttons set them to a ready-made curve.<br><br>This line also has <b>dropTarget</b> set for any widget, so you can move the cubes onto it.",
    "layer": -3
  },
  "text3": {
    "id": "text3",
    "x": 830,
    "y": 400,
    "width": 360,
    "z": 9732,
    "movable": false,
    "movableInEdit": false,
    "css": {
      "font-size": "22px"
    },
    "html": "This line has a width of 0 so it is invisible - only the four holders above show where it runs. They still follow its curve. By default the stops are evenly spaced (<b>autoSpaceStops</b>: true, <b>Distribute evenly</b>) and rotate (<b>rotateStops</b>: true, <b>Turn stops to follow the line</b>) to follow it. Move the end points, control points, and add and delete stops to see this in action. The line lists its attached stops in its <b>stops</b> property. You can add stops in the JSON Editor, by dropping a widget that matches the line's <b>dropTarget</b> onto it (like the dashed blue one to the left), or with <b>Add stop</b> in the &quotEdit Widgets&quot tab. Stops sometimes also get a <b>lineOriginalRotation</b> property which should be ignored as it is automatically managed as the line moves or changes shape.",
    "layer": -3
  },
  "text4": {
    "id": "text4",
    "x": 1299,
    "y": 551,
    "width": 300,
    "z": 9732,
    "movable": false,
    "movableInEdit": false,
    "css": {
      "font-size": "22px"
    },
    "html": "These lines use <b>connectEnd</b> and <b>connectStart</b> (<b>Connect points</b>) to attach their ends to basic widgets - the black circles. Drag those around to see the lines move together. The rotation rule is simple: a stop wider than it is tall (the red line's holders) is turned to the direction the line runs in there; a portrait or square one keeps its own rotation. The orange line turns that off with <b>rotateStops</b>: false. The green line uses specific spacing instead of automatic spacing.",
    "layer": -3
  },
